  "result": "I've written the lesson and I'm wrapping up with a summary for you.\n\n**Lesson:** `lessons/0001-context-and-memory-in-an-agent.html` (objective O3, 10 minutes)\n\nIt covers:\n- **Context window:** the model sees only what your code sends on each call. The history is resent every step, so input cost grows with each step. It also introduces context rot.\n- **What survives:** between steps, only what your code keeps in the message list. Between sessions, only what it saves. Compaction is the fix when a session gets long.\n- **Notes as memory:** the example is a small per-student training record loaded at session start.\n- **Retrieval:** searching the manuals up front versus letting the model fetch pages just in time.\n- **Worked example:** a laser-cutter request, with token counts I made up for illustration, followed by a faded \"your turn\".\n\nIt also has a warm-up recall on the agent loop, one figure, two quick checks and a summary.\n\nTwo things to know:\n- **Length:** I didn't count the words, so I can't confirm it lands near 1,500. Tell me if it reads long or short.\n- **Sources:** the facts come from the Anthropic engineering post on context engineering, which I opened. The Claude docs context-windows page is linked from search results only. I added both to `RESOURCES.md`.\n\nI also noted the lesson in `NOTES.md`. I didn't write a quiz. Ask me anything about the lesson in the chat, and use `/quiz` when you want a quiz.\n\nOne question for you: do you want the next lesson to be sub-agents and splitting work, or workflows versus agents? Your mission lists both.",
